# This is a commit on `main`, not a branch head, and the distinction is the
# point rather than pedantry. `.github/actions/clone-counterpart` fetches a raw
# SHA and checks out FETCH_HEAD, so a branch head resolves perfectly well and
# then stops being an ancestor of main the moment anything rewrites the branch,
# and it keeps resolving through refs/pull/N/head even after the branch is
# deleted. Nothing would ever fail. CI would quietly test a tree nobody has.
# tests/counterpart_pinning.rs only checks the SHA's shape and the clone action
# only checks that HEAD equals the pin, so neither of them would notice.
# Confirmed at bump time rather than assumed: `gh api
# repos/libviprs/libviprs/commits/main` answers exactly this SHA, so the pin is
# main's own tip.
